      "note": "Andreas (vol. 2, p. 358): the mansard's extreme height twenty-three feet (7.0 m) above the cornice and ninety-nine feet from the basement floor; Chicago Illustrated gives one hundred feet in height. The wall head stands about 22 m over the street in the photograph (scaled on the 93-foot front), so the roof is built 6.6 m with its 0.4 m cresting over the cornice, the top 29 m over the street.",
      "geometry": "simplified"
